alSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"Harris argues that the greatest loss from constant online connection is the loss of absence itself—silence, wonder, and solitude—reducing them over time despite gains in information and connection.","confidence":0.74},"academicOrientation":{"score":45,"confidence":0.46},"profileConfidence":0.72,"model":"gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"e782448800ec3fc6a1a4bfefb8ea5612d1711f3e18a9687828a017fb96c97016","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"Society & Culture","secondarySubjects":["Technology","Politics & Government"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book explores the impacts of constant digital connectivity on social life and culture, making Society & Culture the dominant subject, while also touching on themes related to technology and governance.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"2b1d0fadbe52ef67f6be9ddd3b1acc4ba4904681840e3ea1525e868d76f97e59","summary":"\"Only one generation in history (ours) will experience life both with and without the Internet. For everyone who follows us, online life will simply be the air they breathe. Today, we revel in ubiquitous information and constant connection, rarely stopping to consider the implications for our logged-on lives. Michael Harris chronicles this massive shift, exploring what we've gained--and lost--in the bargain. In this eloquent and thought-provoking book, Harris argues that our greatest loss has been that of absence itself--of silence, wonder, and solitude. It's a surprisingly precious commodity, and one we have less of every year. Drawing on a vast trove of research and scores of interviews with global experts, Harris explores this \"loss of lack\" in chapters devoted to every corner of our lives, from sex and commerce to memory and attention span.","publicationYear":2014,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","